00:29:56.440 --> 00:30:00.240
>> One line that was given to me early on

00:29:58.600 --> 00:30:03.160
in the show, probably in its second

00:30:00.240 --> 00:30:05.760
year, was this idea that opportunities

00:30:03.160 --> 00:30:07.840
become visible once you're in motion.

00:30:05.760 --> 00:30:10.320
And at the time, I dismissed it as it's

00:30:07.840 --> 00:30:12.520
a little hippie woo-woo kind of stuff,

00:30:10.320 --> 00:30:15.040
but I've seen it come true and over and

00:30:12.520 --> 00:30:17.400
over again. It's this idea that action

00:30:15.040 --> 00:30:19.880
breeds action and action breeds

00:30:17.400 --> 00:30:21.320
conversations. You improve your network

00:30:19.880 --> 00:30:23.520
and that conversation with somebody else

00:30:21.320 --> 00:30:25.000
is sparks this next idea. Going back to

00:30:23.520 --> 00:30:27.200
the virtual assistant business, I was

00:30:25.000 --> 00:30:29.640
working on a completely unrelated side

00:30:27.200 --> 00:30:31.400
project in the wine space, which I had

00:30:29.640 --> 00:30:32.800
no business being in. I don't know

00:30:31.400 --> 00:30:34.040
anything about wine. I don't really

00:30:32.800 --> 00:30:36.480
drink wine. I don't really care anything

00:30:34.040 --> 00:30:37.960
about wine, but it was

00:30:36.480 --> 00:30:40.360
for whatever reason, I came across this

00:30:37.960 --> 00:30:42.080
wine club review site. And I looked up

00:30:40.360 --> 00:30:43.560
some of their affiliate programs and

00:30:42.080 --> 00:30:45.160
it's like, "Oh, this site is probably

00:30:43.560 --> 00:30:47.720
making pretty money. It was making good

00:30:45.160 --> 00:30:50.320
money. It ranked well for such and such

00:30:47.720 --> 00:30:52.560
wine club keyword plus review or plus

00:30:50.320 --> 00:30:54.280
reviews." I was like, "Oh, that's really

00:30:52.560 --> 00:30:56.160
interesting. What could I pivot that

00:30:54.280 --> 00:30:58.040
to?" We've talked about rip, pivot, and

00:30:56.160 --> 00:31:01.160
jam. Rip, taking somebody else's

00:30:58.040 --> 00:31:02.840
business idea, pivoting to your own

00:31:01.160 --> 00:31:05.440
niche, your own expertise, like whatever

00:31:02.840 --> 00:31:07.640
you're curious or obsessed about, and

00:31:05.440 --> 00:31:10.520
then jam, go and do the work, do the

00:31:07.640 --> 00:31:13.800
hustle. And in my case, it was like

00:31:10.520 --> 00:31:16.240
using the exact same WordPress theme as

00:31:13.800 --> 00:31:17.440
this wine club review site to build out

00:31:16.240 --> 00:31:19.040
the first version of the virtual

00:31:17.440 --> 00:31:22.320
assistant review site.

00:31:19.040 --> 00:31:24.960
And it was a clear pivot that that ended

00:31:22.320 --> 00:31:26.760
up working out really well. So, that is

00:31:24.960 --> 00:31:28.560
something that I was excited about. It

00:31:26.760 --> 00:31:31.080
was an opportunity that became visible

00:31:28.560 --> 00:31:33.320
once I was in motion. Had I never built

00:31:31.080 --> 00:31:35.120
this silly-looking wine site, never

00:31:33.320 --> 00:31:36.560
would have come across that opportunity.

00:31:35.120 --> 00:31:38.200
And it's true, it's come up over and

00:31:36.560 --> 00:31:41.080
over again from so many guests. The

00:31:38.200 --> 00:31:42.960
business that they start with is often

00:31:41.080 --> 00:31:44.640
not the business that they end up doing,

00:31:42.960 --> 00:31:46.600
but it's what got the ball rolling. We

00:31:44.640 --> 00:31:48.720
have a line in our house, my wife and I,

00:31:46.600 --> 00:31:50.440
where we say the weather in Turkey. And

00:31:48.720 --> 00:31:52.480
where it came from was we have this

00:31:50.440 --> 00:31:54.120
upcoming trip to Istanbul and I'm

00:31:52.480 --> 00:31:55.600
looking ahead at the 10-day forecast and

00:31:54.120 --> 00:31:57.080
I'm like, "Oh, shoot, it's it looks like

00:31:55.600 --> 00:31:58.640
it's going to rain while we're there."

00:31:57.080 --> 00:32:00.560
And she's, "Whatever, it's we're not

00:31:58.640 --> 00:32:02.280
canceling the trip. This

00:32:00.560 --> 00:32:03.600
the weather in Turkey." It's a call to

00:32:02.280 --> 00:32:04.880
control the controllables. I could bring

00:32:03.600 --> 00:32:06.360
a rain jacket, but I'm probably not

00:32:04.880 --> 00:32:08.600
going to cancel this trip, right?

00:32:06.360 --> 00:32:10.480
There's a line where you can't stop the

00:32:08.600 --> 00:32:12.040
waves, but you can learn how to surf. It

00:32:10.480 --> 00:32:13.800
is a call to

00:32:12.040 --> 00:32:16.000
control the elements of your business

00:32:13.800 --> 00:32:17.560
that you have control over and doing

00:32:16.000 --> 00:32:19.520
your best to weather the storms that

00:32:17.560 --> 00:32:23.120
come your way, but recognizing that

00:32:19.520 --> 00:32:25.240
sometimes stuff just happens. Now, I've

00:32:23.120 --> 00:32:27.640
told the story before of literally my

00:32:25.240 --> 00:32:29.080
first day of self-employment after I

00:32:27.640 --> 00:32:31.880
gave my notice at work and turned in the

00:32:29.080 --> 00:32:33.880
keys to my company car, that's the day

00:32:31.880 --> 00:32:35.280
that Google decides to shut down my

00:32:33.880 --> 00:32:38.472
advertising account that the shoe

00:32:35.280 --> 00:32:38.520
business relied on. And it was like,

00:32:38.472 --> 00:32:40.600
>> [sighs and gasps]

00:32:38.520 --> 00:32:43.120
>> Okay, deep breath. You go through the

00:32:40.600 --> 00:32:45.200
seven stages of anger and denial and

00:32:43.120 --> 00:32:48.280
ultimately acceptance and saying, "What

00:32:45.200 --> 00:32:49.640
can I control here? Okay, I can control

00:32:48.280 --> 00:32:51.880
the landing pages. I can make some

00:32:49.640 --> 00:32:54.560
improvements based on what I think they

00:32:51.880 --> 00:32:57.200
might want to see." And ultimately, it

00:32:54.560 --> 00:32:58.840
was definitely a stressful summer and an

00:32:57.200 --> 00:33:00.920
expensive one in redeveloping a lot of

00:32:58.840 --> 00:33:03.480
the site, but ultimately, they came back

00:33:00.920 --> 00:33:05.080
and said, "Okay, we made an error.

00:33:03.480 --> 00:33:07.120
You're good to go. The ad account is

00:33:05.080 --> 00:33:09.600
back on." So, it was an example of,

00:33:07.120 --> 00:33:11.840
"Okay, their decision is somewhat

00:33:09.600 --> 00:33:13.360
outside of my control, but I can control

00:33:11.840 --> 00:33:15.360
the user experience on the site and

00:33:13.360 --> 00:33:16.920
hopefully get it back into their good

00:33:15.360 --> 00:33:19.040
graces and be able to run that business

00:33:16.920 --> 00:33:21.560
for several more years." The next idea I

00:33:19.040 --> 00:33:23.880
want to present is this one called

00:33:21.560 --> 00:33:25.679
sharpen your pencil. And this comes from

00:33:23.880 --> 00:33:28.000
my dad when he'd be helping me with

00:33:25.679 --> 00:33:29.600
homework as a kid, he'd come over to the

00:33:28.000 --> 00:33:31.160
kitchen table and be like, "Look look at

00:33:29.600 --> 00:33:32.960
this thing. Go sharpen your pencil. You

00:33:31.160 --> 00:33:34.400
can't do good work with a dull pencil."

00:33:32.960 --> 00:33:36.040
I was like, "Come on, just help me out

00:33:34.400 --> 00:33:37.320
with this math problem." Whatever. It

00:33:36.040 --> 00:33:39.388
doesn't matter. He's like, "No, the

00:33:37.320 --> 00:33:41.520
tools you work with matter." And

00:33:39.388 --> 00:33:45.000
[snorts] this is a call to get a little

00:33:41.520 --> 00:33:48.280
bit better every day. It is looking for

00:33:45.000 --> 00:33:51.160
that 1% improvement, that little slight

00:33:48.280 --> 00:33:53.679
optimization, that better, smarter,

00:33:51.160 --> 00:33:55.560
faster way to get things done. My

00:33:53.679 --> 00:33:58.480
virtual assistant back in the day, we

00:33:55.560 --> 00:34:00.800
were writing out hundreds of text ads

00:33:58.480 --> 00:34:02.280
for these specific models of shoes. And

00:34:00.800 --> 00:34:03.240
she's, "Woah, have you heard of this

00:34:02.280 --> 00:34:05.360
little

00:34:03.240 --> 00:34:06.480
text expander app?" I was like, "No,

00:34:05.360 --> 00:34:08.440
what's that?" She's like, "Oh, let me

00:34:06.480 --> 00:34:10.000
show you. It accelerates things. Oh, and

00:34:08.440 --> 00:34:11.600
by the way, these campaigns are slow to

00:34:10.000 --> 00:34:13.080
load because of so many products in

00:34:11.600 --> 00:34:15.320
there. What if you kept four different

00:34:13.080 --> 00:34:17.640
tabs open?" And now we can do this four

00:34:15.320 --> 00:34:19.159
times. It's like just these little micro

00:34:17.640 --> 00:34:21.240
improvements are the equivalent of

00:34:19.159 --> 00:34:23.200
sharpening the pencil in my case. And

00:34:21.240 --> 00:34:25.520
try and think of other examples when it

00:34:23.200 --> 00:34:28.359
comes to the podcast, like little

00:34:25.520 --> 00:34:32.320
workflow improvements and right now

00:34:28.359 --> 00:34:34.520
building out mini AI agents to do

00:34:32.320 --> 00:34:36.720
certain parts of the process for me.

00:34:34.520 --> 00:34:38.879
It's like even after 13 years of doing

00:34:36.720 --> 00:34:40.159
it, it's like there's still little

00:34:38.879 --> 00:34:42.120
performance improvements. There's still

00:34:40.159 --> 00:34:43.640
little ways to sharpen the pencil every

00:34:42.120 --> 00:34:45.520
day. And I think you'll find the same in

00:34:43.640 --> 00:34:48.240
your business. A lot of entrepreneurs

00:34:45.520 --> 00:34:49.840
have the tendency to reinvest almost

00:34:48.240 --> 00:34:52.480
everything back into their business and

00:34:49.840 --> 00:34:53.760
you see this a lot in e-commerce cuz we

00:34:52.480 --> 00:34:55.000
have something that hits, I need more

00:34:53.760 --> 00:34:57.000
inventory. And now I've got all my

00:34:55.000 --> 00:34:59.600
capital tied up in inventory and growth

00:34:57.000 --> 00:35:01.960
sometimes for years. And so, the idea

00:34:59.600 --> 00:35:03.880
that I want to present is it's okay to

00:35:01.960 --> 00:35:06.400
take some chips off the table. Nobody

00:35:03.880 --> 00:35:09.000
ever went broke taking a profit. And in

00:35:06.400 --> 00:35:10.400
my case, I'm trying to reinvest some of

00:35:09.000 --> 00:35:12.080
the earnings back into the business, of

00:35:10.400 --> 00:35:13.880
course, because it's probably the best

00:35:12.080 --> 00:35:15.680
ROI that you're ever going to see. If

00:35:13.880 --> 00:35:18.880
you can get something that's compounding

00:35:15.680 --> 00:35:21.040
20, 30, 50% a year, like you'd be crazy

00:35:18.880 --> 00:35:22.880
not to feed that machine a little bit.

00:35:21.040 --> 00:35:25.680
But at the same time, like try to take

00:35:22.880 --> 00:35:28.520
some chips off the table and invest that

00:35:25.680 --> 00:35:31.000
into dividend-paying stocks, into real

00:35:28.520 --> 00:35:32.200
estate, into something where if I can

00:35:31.000 --> 00:35:34.600
get paid once, I want to make that

00:35:32.200 --> 00:35:35.840
dollar work for me over and over again.

00:35:34.600 --> 00:35:37.920
Sometimes it's going to be a very small

00:35:35.840 --> 00:35:39.640
percentage, but like socking a little

00:35:37.920 --> 00:35:42.320
bit of that away and trying to stack up

00:35:39.640 --> 00:35:44.280
some truly passive cash flow in that

00:35:42.320 --> 00:35:45.840
way. So, that's my call to take some

00:35:44.280 --> 00:35:46.960
chips off the table when it makes sense.

00:35:45.840 --> 00:35:48.800
And for me, maybe the example was

00:35:46.960 --> 00:35:51.960
selling the virtual assistant site in

00:35:48.800 --> 00:35:53.800
2020. It was like my time to work on it

00:35:51.960 --> 00:35:55.359
had diminished and I was like, "Okay, I

00:35:53.800 --> 00:35:57.720
don't want to see it start to decline,

00:35:55.359 --> 00:35:59.200
so maybe it would be a good time to hand

00:35:57.720 --> 00:36:00.720
off the reins to the next owner, to the

00:35:59.200 --> 00:36:02.000
next buyer." One question that comes up

00:36:00.720 --> 00:36:04.080
quite a bit is how do you know when it's

00:36:02.000 --> 00:36:05.720
time to throw in the towel on a side

00:36:04.080 --> 00:36:07.240
hustle? And the answer for me has

00:36:05.720 --> 00:36:09.480
historically been

00:36:07.240 --> 00:36:11.440
when you come to dread the work. The

00:36:09.480 --> 00:36:12.880
last thing that you need is a second job

00:36:11.440 --> 00:36:14.800
that you hate, that you really don't

00:36:12.880 --> 00:36:16.480
look forward to doing. And there's a

00:36:14.800 --> 00:36:18.960
fine line between kind of like pushing

00:36:16.480 --> 00:36:20.800
through the tough parts and okay, not

00:36:18.960 --> 00:36:22.920
every day is going to be rainbows and

00:36:20.800 --> 00:36:24.960
unicorns, but if you start to see a

00:36:22.920 --> 00:36:26.600
pattern where you're like, "This is not

00:36:24.960 --> 00:36:27.960
enjoyable. I'm not seeing the results

00:36:26.600 --> 00:36:29.440
that I want."

00:36:27.960 --> 00:36:31.240
You don't need this in your life, right?

00:36:29.440 --> 00:36:32.680
There's a business ideas are like buses.

00:36:31.240 --> 00:36:34.920
There's always another one coming. Yeah,

00:36:32.680 --> 00:36:36.480
I think it's totally fine. There's a

00:36:34.920 --> 00:36:38.040
"Oh, quitters never win and winners

00:36:36.480 --> 00:36:39.359
never quit." It's like winners quit all

00:36:38.040 --> 00:36:41.520
the time, right? If something is not

00:36:39.359 --> 00:36:43.520
serving you, it's totally okay to quit

00:36:41.520 --> 00:36:45.320
that or even put it on pause, put it on

00:36:43.520 --> 00:36:47.640
the back burner, and try and figure out

00:36:45.320 --> 00:36:50.640
the next stage. So, just want to put

00:36:47.640 --> 00:36:52.200
that out there as permission to not work

00:36:50.640 --> 00:36:54.520
yourself, paint yourself into a corner

00:36:52.200 --> 00:36:55.920
where I'm now I'm trapped doing this

00:36:54.520 --> 00:36:57.720
thing. Where it's No, you're still in

00:36:55.920 --> 00:37:00.120
the driver's seat and if it's not

00:36:57.720 --> 00:37:02.200
serving you, it's okay to put a pause,

00:37:00.120 --> 00:37:03.600
to throw in the towel, or to say, "Okay,

00:37:02.200 --> 00:37:05.080
I'm going to pivot and I'm going to try

00:37:03.600 --> 00:37:07.160
something else." One of my most

00:37:05.080 --> 00:37:09.680
important side hustle habits is this

00:37:07.160 --> 00:37:11.440
practice of nightly I'd done this

00:37:09.680 --> 00:37:13.160
exercise. What did I get done today? Did

00:37:11.440 --> 00:37:15.160
I do work that mattered? Give yourself

00:37:13.160 --> 00:37:16.800
permission to celebrate some wins and

00:37:15.160 --> 00:37:19.040
some progress along the way because

00:37:16.800 --> 00:37:20.960
entrepreneurship can be a lonely road,

00:37:19.040 --> 00:37:22.480
right? And nobody else knows what you're

00:37:20.960 --> 00:37:25.000
going through. And so, you want to take

00:37:22.480 --> 00:37:26.440
stock of Okay, I checked this off my

00:37:25.000 --> 00:37:28.840
list. I got this off my list. This was

00:37:26.440 --> 00:37:31.800
important to me. I got this done. And

00:37:28.840 --> 00:37:34.160
being really intentional both on that

00:37:31.800 --> 00:37:36.520
progress side, what did I do, and also

00:37:34.160 --> 00:37:38.840
for me really important is to say what

00:37:36.520 --> 00:37:41.240
am I going to do tomorrow? That way, I

00:37:38.840 --> 00:37:43.880
know exactly what to do cuz yesterday

00:37:41.240 --> 00:37:46.320
Nick told me so, in what order even,

00:37:43.880 --> 00:37:48.080
before I start reacting to somebody

00:37:46.320 --> 00:37:51.480
else's agenda. Like what am I going to

00:37:48.080 --> 00:37:55.000
do proactively for me and my business

00:37:51.480 --> 00:37:56.800
before going into a reactive mode. So,

00:37:55.000 --> 00:37:58.359
tracking your progress and then taking a

00:37:56.800 --> 00:37:59.800
moment for gratitude, that's all stuff

00:37:58.359 --> 00:38:02.000
that I have baked into my three question

00:37:59.800 --> 00:38:03.840
journal. It's what I get done today,

00:38:02.000 --> 00:38:05.000
what am I grateful for, and what are my

00:38:03.840 --> 00:38:06.520
what are my top priorities for tomorrow?

00:38:05.000 --> 00:38:08.120
What am I going to do tomorrow to win

00:38:06.520 --> 00:38:09.960
the next day? And John Lee Dumas gave

00:38:08.120 --> 00:38:12.720
the line, "You win tomorrow today." And

00:38:09.960 --> 00:38:15.240
it's the same idea of doing a little bit

00:38:12.720 --> 00:38:16.680
of advanced planning cuz the to-do list

00:38:15.240 --> 00:38:18.520
is never done, right? And that's one of

00:38:16.680 --> 00:38:21.040
my struggles as an entrepreneur. There's

00:38:18.520 --> 00:38:23.240
always more ideas than there are hours

00:38:21.040 --> 00:38:24.560
in the day, but trying to be

00:38:23.240 --> 00:38:25.800
intentional. Okay, this is what I think

00:38:24.560 --> 00:38:28.320
I can get done. These are my most

00:38:25.800 --> 00:38:30.560
important things to go ahead. So, be

00:38:28.320 --> 00:38:32.480
intentional about celebrating your wins

00:38:30.560 --> 00:38:34.840
and making some progress. And finally, I

00:38:32.480 --> 00:38:37.320
think the goal is to find a side hustle

00:38:34.840 --> 00:38:38.840
where you can I have a goal in mind, of

00:38:37.320 --> 00:38:41.200
course. I think it's important to set

00:38:38.840 --> 00:38:43.640
the GPS where you want to go, but then

00:38:41.200 --> 00:38:46.120
also appreciate the day-to-day journey.

00:38:43.640 --> 00:38:49.040
The journey really is the destination.

00:38:46.120 --> 00:38:50.920
Finding work that you enjoy, we've heard

00:38:49.040 --> 00:38:52.400
that the secret to happiness is

00:38:50.920 --> 00:38:55.000
meaningful work plus meaningful

00:38:52.400 --> 00:38:57.560
relationships plus a sense of progress,

00:38:55.000 --> 00:39:00.120
a journey toward mastery, like getting

00:38:57.560 --> 00:39:01.800
better at your craft. Like Joe Saul-Sehy

00:39:00.120 --> 00:39:03.720
from Stacking Benjamins, he always gives

00:39:01.800 --> 00:39:06.600
me this line of "I'm embarrassed by the

00:39:03.720 --> 00:39:08.400
work I did a year ago and a year from

00:39:06.600 --> 00:39:11.200
now, I hope to be embarrassed by the

00:39:08.400 --> 00:39:14.480
work that I did today." But he's on that

00:39:11.200 --> 00:39:16.000
journey that every day I get to do work

00:39:14.480 --> 00:39:19.120
that I love. And yes, there's a

00:39:16.000 --> 00:39:21.000
destination in mind. This is what I want

00:39:19.120 --> 00:39:23.720
in terms of financial goals for my

00:39:21.000 --> 00:39:25.640
family or an end goal for my customers,

00:39:23.720 --> 00:39:26.760
but along the way, I'm going to enjoy

00:39:25.640 --> 00:39:29.640
the moment. I'm going to take a moment

00:39:26.760 --> 00:39:31.400
to look around and appreciate that this

00:39:29.640 --> 00:39:33.359
is pretty cool that I get to do this

00:39:31.400 --> 00:39:35.480
kind of work. So, really appreciate you

00:39:33.359 --> 00:39:37.280
tuning in. If you find any value in the

00:39:35.480 --> 00:39:39.080
show, the greatest compliment is to

00:39:37.280 --> 00:39:41.240
share with a friend. So, fire off that

00:39:39.080 --> 00:39:42.640
text message, let them know about it.

00:39:41.240 --> 00:39:44.280
Thanks so much for watching and make

00:39:42.640 --> 00:39:47.320
sure to subscribe for more money making

00:39:44.280 --> 00:39:47.320
ideas. Cheers.

